DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The SHA512_ functions calculate a 512-bit cryptographic checksum (digest)
     for any number of input bytes.  A cryptographic checksum is a one-way
     hash function; that is, it is computationally impractical to find the in‐
     put corresponding to a particular output.  This net result is a
     “fingerprint” of the input-data, which does not disclose the actual in‐
     put.

     The SSHHAA551122__IInniitt(), SSHHAA551122__UUppddaattee(), and SSHHAA551122__FFiinnaall() functions are the
     core functions.  Allocate an _S_H_A_5_1_2___C_T_X, initialize it with
     SSHHAA551122__IInniitt(), run over the data with SSHHAA551122__UUppddaattee(), and finally ex‐
     tract the result using SSHHAA551122__FFiinnaall(), which will also erase the
     _S_H_A_5_1_2___C_T_X.

     SSHHAA551122__EEnndd() is a wrapper for SSHHAA551122__FFiinnaall() which converts the return
     value to a 129-character (including the terminating '\0') ASCII string
     which represents the 512 bits in hexadecimal.

     SSHHAA551122__FFiillee() calculates the digest of a file, and uses SSHHAA551122__EEnndd() to
     return the result.  If the file cannot be opened, a null pointer is re‐
     turned.  SSHHAA551122__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() is similar to SSHHAA551122__FFiillee(), but it only cal‐
     culates the digest over a byte-range of the file specified, starting at
     _o_f_f_s_e_t and spanning _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes.  If the _l_e_n_g_t_h parameter is specified
     as 0, or more than the length of the remaining part of the file,
     SSHHAA551122__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() calculates the digest from _o_f_f_s_e_t to the end of file.
     SSHHAA551122__DDaattaa() calculates the digest of a chunk of data in memory, and
     uses SSHHAA551122__EEnndd() to return the result.

     When using SSHHAA551122__EEnndd(), SSHHAA551122__FFiillee(), or SSHHAA551122__DDaattaa(), the _b_u_f argu‐
     ment can be a null pointer, in which case the returned string is allo‐
     cated with malloc(3) and subsequently must be explicitly deallocated us‐
     ing free(3) after use.  If the _b_u_f argument is non-null it must point to
     at least 129 characters of buffer space.

     The SHA384_, SHA512_224, and SHA512_256_ functions are identical to the
     SHA512_ functions except they use a different initial hash value and the
     output is truncated to 384, 224, and 256 bits respectively.

     SSHHAA338844__EEnndd() is a wrapper for SSHHAA338844__FFiinnaall() which converts the return
     value to a 97-character (including the terminating '\0') ASCII string
     which represents the 384 bits in hexadecimal.

     SSHHAA551122__222244__EEnndd() is a wrapper for SSHHAA551122__FFiinnaall() which converts the re‐
     turn value to a 57-character (including the terminating '\0') ASCII
     string which represents the 224 bits in hexadecimal.

     SSHHAA551122__222244__EEnndd() is a wrapper for SSHHAA551122__FFiinnaall() which converts the re‐
     turn value to a 57-character (including the terminating '\0') ASCII
     string which represents the 224 bits in hexadecimal.

     SSHHAA551122__225566__EEnndd() is a wrapper for SSHHAA551122__FFiinnaall() which converts the re‐
     turn value to a 65-character (including the terminating '\0') ASCII
     string which represents the 256 bits in hexadecimal.

EERRRROORRSS
     The SSHHAA551122__EEnndd() function called with a null buf argument may fail and
     return NULL if:

     [ENOMEM]           Insufficient storage space is available.

     The SSHHAA551122__FFiillee() and SSHHAA551122__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() may return NULL when underlying
     open(2), fstat(2), lseek(2), or SHA512_End(3) fail.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     md4(3), md5(3), ripemd(3), sha(3), sha256(3), sha512(3), skein(3)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     These functions appeared in FreeBSD 9.0.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     The core hash routines were implemented by Colin Percival based on the
     published FIPS 180-2 standard.

BBUUGGSS
     No method is known to exist which finds two files having the same hash
     value, nor to find a file with a specific hash value.  There is on the
     other hand no guarantee that such a method does not exist.

